  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Scope**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the initial scope for the \"SalesFlow Pro Task Board\" page. I\'ve pinpointed its core functionality: a desktop-friendly Kanban interface with horizontally scrolling columns. The essential elements seem to be \"Task Board\" as the main header, and filters for Customer, PIC, and Priority. I\'m aiming for a clear, intuitive design.\n\n\n**Expanding the Details**\n\nI\'ve been fleshing out the specifics of the task board, particularly the column structure and task card UI. I\'ve decided on four columns: TODO, IN PROGRESS, REVIEW, and COMPLETED. Each column will display a title and task count. Task cards will include the essential details such as title, customer, PIC, priority, due date, and a related field. I\'m also planning in drag-and-drop interactions, and activity history logging for task status changes, including an \"+ Add Task\" function. I\'m noting the route `/task-board`. I\'m now verifying the types in `src/types/index.ts` to ensure the correct task status is present.\n\n\n**Refining the Workflow**\n\nI\'m now integrating a \"REVIEW\" column into the task board\'s workflow. I\'m investigating how best to handle this \342\200\223 ideally updating `src/types/index.ts` to include the new status, but I\'ll consider local state if that\'s easier. For drag-and-drop functionality, I plan to leverage native HTML5 drag and drop APIs, avoiding external libraries for now, but I will check `package.json` just in case. Finally, I\'ll figure out how to add activity logging, targeting either `DataService` or `localStorage`. My plan includes verifying and updating the task status types, and I\'ll create the `TaskBoardPage.tsx` file.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 456058221
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
