  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Objective**\n\nOkay, I\'m starting by zeroing in on the core goal: building this \"Task Report\" page within SalesFlow Pro. I\'ve broken down the project by first, recognizing the intent and then figuring out what it is going to entail. I\'m focusing on the main aspects like the title and figuring out what filters are going to be necessary. Right now, I am looking at the necessities such as the title, as well as the filtering options that will be required. I\'m taking the pieces, and beginning the implementation phase.\n\n\n**Outlining Report Components**\n\nI\'ve moved on to fleshing out the specific components for the \"Task Report\" page. I\'ve sketched out the KPIs (Total, Completed, In Progress, Overdue, and Completion Rate) and chart types (bar, pie/donut, and line/area) that seem most appropriate. I\'m also planning a detailed table, including overdue task highlighting, and thinking about the \"Task Report\" navigation, including the route in the `App.tsx` and adding a new file. Lastly, I will build out the skeleton using the sales and visits report pages as a guide.\n\n\n**Adding Task Highlighting**\n\nI\'m now implementing the overdue task highlighting logic; it will check statuses and due dates. Next, I\'ll update `App.tsx` and `Sidebar.tsx` to include the route and link for \"Task Report\" page. After that, I will run the standard linting and build commands to be sure everything is ship-shape.\n\nI\'m getting into the meat of it, drafting the component. I\'m focusing on the imports and setting up mock data for KPIs and various chart types. I\'m preparing all of the necessary things, such as component structure, data preparation, as well as the imports necessary for the design. I\'ve designed the page\'s components and table data and components. The header is setup to include a button for export.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 53
      nanos: 762506177
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
